A||||2||Steven||Plenum refinish?||||1||
Z||000000||Steven||05-08-2001||05:30 PM||||I stopped on the way home last night, and picked up a couple of cans of red spray paint for the valve covers and the plenum. I bought VHT brand high temp engine paint. My questions are:<P>1. Has anyone used this, and how happy are you with the color?<P>2. Did you tape off the sections of covers and plenum that are not painted, or paint the whole thing, and sand off the paint where it is normally silver, or wipe the paint off before it fully set with paint thinner?<P>3. Did you prime it first?<P>TIA<BR>------------------<BR> 85 Fiero GT<BR> 3.2 Liter Under Construction<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||05-08-2001||06:10 PM||||I used Plasticote engine enamel (500 degree), universal red.  I thoroughly cleaned/degreased everything with Prep-Sol (2 or 3 times), scuffed it up with something like #240 paper and sprayed.  No primer, no masking.  I just avoided putting alot of paint on the "high spots".  After they were completely dry, I used a orbital sander to clean off the "high spots", then aluminum polished them.  The paint looks and is holding up great.  The bright aluminum "high spots" still need periodic polishing to keep them really shiney.<P>I have noticed that some of the very high temp paints get very flat (no shine) after they cure.  I would avoid these paints!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/86gt07.jpg">  <P>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 05-08-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steven||05-08-2001||06:19 PM||||Man that is exactly what I needed to see and hear (well read I guess). Thanks. By the way the look great.<P>------------------<BR> 85 Fiero GT<BR> 3.2 Liter Under Construction||||reg||1
